<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eumenes II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/197-bce">197</a>–159 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eumenes II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> in the early second century BCE and an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His cavalry supported the Roman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE, which inflicted a heavy defeat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and Pergamon received much of Seleucid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii.jpg" alt="Eumenes II" width="255" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eumene_II_detto_giovane_comandante,_da_villa_dei_papiri,_peristilio_quadrato.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eumenes II Soter was the eldest of four sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I Soter</a> and Queen Apollonis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalid dynasty</a>. He was presumably born before 220 BCE. He refused to marry a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> when he saw that Antiochus was about to go to war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and he married Stratonice, daughter of Ariarathes IV, king of Cappadocia. Their son was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Eumenes became king in 197 BCE. Like his father he worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> against Antigonid and especially Seleucid expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a> he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the Achaean League against the Spartan tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis</a>. In the Roman-Seleucid War he warned Rome of Antiochus's ambitions by sending his brother Attalus II ahead, and then sided with the Romans. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE he commanded the right flank, and his fleet fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> at sea.</p><p>By the Peace of Apamea in 188 BCE he received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>, parts of Lycia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a>. By dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> between their allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> made sure that neither would grow too powerful.</p><p>In 183 BCE he fought the Bithynian king Prusias I, was at first defeated, and won after receiving Roman support. He also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus">Pharnaces I of Pontus</a>, who sued for peace around 179 BCE after suffering losses.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> he helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pydna">Battle of Pydna</a>. Afterwards the Romans suspected him of conspiring with Perseus. He sent his brother Attalus II to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with congratulations, and in 167 BCE the Romans made an abortive attempt to put Attalus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> throne. Eumenes set out to plead his case in person but was ordered to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as soon as he landed at Brundusium. Ties of kinship proved strong, and he remained king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>When Eumenes's health weakened, Attalus II became co-ruler in 160 BCE. Because Eumenes's son was a minor, Attalus took the throne and in 158 BCE married Stratonice. Eumenes raised his state to a powerful monarchy, and under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> became a flourishing city that welcomed scholars, among them Crates of Mallus, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> school of criticism. He adorned it with splendid buildings, including the great altar with the frieze of the Battle of the Giants, and expanded the Library at Pergamon, one of the great libraries of the ancient world and traditionally associated with the creation of parchment. He also built a stoa on the Athenian acropolis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eumenes II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His elder son Perseus (179–168 BCE) restored Macedonian prestige among the Greeks, which Rome's ally <b>Eumenes II of Pergamon</b> denounced before the Senate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…in catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Magnesia in 190 BCE, where a Roman army of perhaps 30,000, supported by the Pergamene cavalry of <b>Eumenes II</b>, routed a Seleucid force of some 70,000 and exposed the tactical limitations of the phalanx against the more flexible Roman manipular legion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Loyal alliance with Rome brought <b>Eumenes II</b> wide lands in Asia Minor after the Peace of Apamea in 188 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a></li></ul></section>
